<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Geotechnical Engineering in San Bernardino</title><meta name="description" content="Geotechnical soil mechanics study in San Bernardino with laboratory testing and field data. We serve the Inland"><link rel="canonical" href="https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Geotechnical Engineering in San Bernardino"><meta property="og:description" content="Geotechnical soil mechanics study in San Bernardino with laboratory testing and field data. We serve the Inland"><meta property="og:url" content="https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Geotechnicalengineering1"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:image" content="https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/img/estudio-mecanica-suelos-1.webp"><meta property="og:image:width" content="1200"><meta property="og:image:height" content="630"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:title" content="Geotechnical Engineering in San Bernardino"><meta name="twitter:description" content="Geotechnical soil mechanics study in San Bernardino with laboratory testing and field data. We serve the Inland Empire with accredited ASTM analysis, liquefaction assessment, and foundation recommenda"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/img/estudio-mecanica-suelos-1.webp">
<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "LocalBusiness", "@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/#org-fc17", "name": "San Bernardino", "url": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/", "email": "info@geotechnicalengineering1.biz", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "addressLocality": "San Bernardino", "addressCountry": "USA"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 34.8253, "longitude": -116.0833}, "areaServed": {"@type": "City", "name": "San Bernardino"}}, {"@type": "Service", "@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/#service-fc17", "serviceType": "Soil mechanics study", "name": "Geotechnical Engineering in San Bernardino", "description": "Geotechnical soil mechanics study in San Bernardino with laboratory testing and field data. We serve the Inland Empire with accredited ASTM analysis, liquefaction assessment, and foundation recommenda", "provider": {"@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/#org-fc17"}, "areaServed": {"@type": "City", "name": "San Bernardino"}, "url": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/"}, {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/", "url": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/", "name": "Geotechnical Engineering in San Bernardino", "inLanguage": "en", "isPartOf": {"@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/#website-fc17"}}, {"@type": "WebSite", "@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/#website-fc17", "url": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/", "name": "San Bernardino", "inLanguage": "en"}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://san-bernardino.geotechnicalengineering1.biz/#faq-fc17", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "How long does a soil mechanics study take from drilling to final report in San Bernardino?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "For a typical commercial lot with three to five borings, we complete the field work in one day and the laboratory phase in seven to ten working days. The final report, which integrates the field logs, lab data, and geotechnical recommendations, is usually delivered within three weeks. Complex projects with consolidation testing or triaxial suites may add five to seven days."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the cost range for a soil mechanics study on a single-family residential lot in San Bernardino?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Based on recent projects in the San Bernardino area, a soil mechanics study for a standard residential parcel runs between US$3,130 and US$5,640. The final figure depends on the number of borings, the depth to competent bearing strata, and the specific lab tests required by the structural engineer."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does the city of San Bernardino require a soil mechanics study for a retaining wall under four feet?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The reference range for this service in San Bernardino is US$3.130 - US$5.640. The final price depends on the project scope and volume."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can you test for expansive soils in San Bernardino?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. We run Atterberg limits and percent passing the No. 200 sieve on every sample from the upper 10 feet. If the plasticity index exceeds 15 and the fines content is above 50%, we perform a swell-consolidation test in the oedometer to measure the percent swell under a 1 psi surcharge. This data is critical for slab-on-grade design in the northern neighborhoods where clay seams are common."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What makes the alluvial soils in the San Bernardino basin different from coastal plain deposits?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The basin fill here is coarser and more angular because the source material comes from the San Bernardino Mountains and is deposited by high-energy streams. That means the friction angles are often higher than typical coastal silts, but the lenses are highly discontinuous. A soil mechanics study in San Bernardino must sample at close vertical intervals to catch the interbedded clay layers that control stability and drainage."}}]}]}</script>
